<!--
    kuna #1288: every memory access, call and return of x86 real-mode code
    printed through a bare segment() call. The SLEIGH spec computes each
    effective address with the user-op segment(seg,off), and the pspec names
    it in a <segmentop> with the p-code res = (zext(base) << 4) + zext(inner).
    Upstream's ActionSegmentize, RuleSegment, SegmentOp and SegmentedResolver
    were never ported, so the op stayed an opaque CALLOTHER: pushes and pops
    were stores through segment(SS,v), no parameter was recovered, a RET
    printed as segment(CS,*(unsigned short *)segment(SS,&Stack0000)), and the
    cs-relative jump table could not be emulated.

    Independently assembled (nasm -f bin, org 0):
      0x00 addtwo: push bp; mov bp,sp; mov ax,[bp+4]; add ax,[bp+6]; pop bp; ret
      0x10 video:  mov ax,0xb800; mov es,ax; mov word [es:0x10],0x741; ret
      0x20 farsum: push bp; mov bp,sp; les bx,[bp+4]; mov ax,[es:bx];
                   add ax,[es:bx+2]; pop bp; ret
      0x30 pick:   n = [bp+4]; if n > 3 return 0; jmp [cs:bx+0x42] over a
                   four-entry table returning 10/20/30/40
      0x70 farlong: mov ax,[cs:0x80]; mov dx,[cs:0x82]; ret (the long at 0x80)
      0x11030 pickfar: pick's bytes again, in code segment 0x1100

    Stage: P3 dataflow (option segmentize, default on). Pass one is option
    segmentize off, the old rendering; pass two is the default. Asserts 1-2
    and 4 count both passes: the bare return statement and the segment()
    store and jump-as-call appear only in pass one. The rest are pass two:
    the frame parameters, the constant segment folded to the linear global
    0xb8010, the far pointer read through LES as one parameter, and the
    recovered switch.

    farlong returns a long read out of global memory in DX:AX. Once the reads
    resolve to globals, the return-pair repair took an unwritten global for a
    leftover and kept AX alone (`return dat_80;`); a global read is a value the
    function computed, so both halves are returned (assert 10).

    pickfar sits in code segment 0x1100, but the spec's CS is the instruction
    address rounded down to 64 KiB, 0x1000, so its jmp [cs:bx+0x42] reads the
    table at 0x10042 instead of 0x11042. The words there send control to
    0x1fff0, outside the image. Such a table is rejected and the jump stays a
    call (asserts 11-12), where following it decoded junk or failed the
    function on the deark-unpacked DAZZLE.EXE.
-->
